hero:
  baslik: 7 Things to Know Before Starting Therapy
  giris: Taking the first step into therapy requires courage. Clarifying your expectations beforehand both reduces anxiety and helps you get the most from therapy.
  rozetSinifi: text-xs bg-sage/20 text-primary font-medium px-3 py-1 rounded-full
blocks:
  - type: metin
    metin: 'Many people view therapy through a distorted lens: expectations such as "You must be very unwell," "You have to tell everything," or "You''ll be fixed in a few sessions" can delay taking that first step. This article is written to clear up that uncertainty.'
  - type: adimlar
    adimlar:
      - baslik: Psychologist–client fit matters more than anything
        metin: Research shows that the greatest factor determining the effectiveness of therapy is not the psychologist's technical knowledge, but the trust built with the client. If you do not feel safe and understood in the first couple of sessions, looking for a different psychologist is both normal and recommended.
      - baslik: The first session is an assessment, not therapy
        metin: In the first session the psychologist tries to understand you and the reason for your visit. Let go of the pressure to "find a solution straight away." This session is also your opportunity to get to know the psychologist and gauge whether you feel comfortable.
      - baslik: Change takes time — and doesn't move in a straight line
        metin: Some weeks you will feel progress; other weeks may feel harder. This is normal. The pattern of "two steps forward, one step back" is very common in therapy. Approaching the process with patience is one of the most important factors in determining the outcome.
      - baslik: You don't have to share everything
        metin: You set your own privacy boundaries. Saying "I don't want to talk about this" is completely valid, and a good psychologist should never push you. Trust builds over time; your disclosures naturally deepen alongside it.
      - baslik: Setting goals speeds progress
        metin: Asking yourself "What do I want to gain from this therapy?" before you begin is very useful. Even if you don't have a clear answer, share that with your psychologist. Shared goals make it easier for each session to stay focused.
      - baslik: Between-session "homework" is part of the process
        metin: Particularly in structured therapies like CBT, your psychologist may suggest exercises or thought journals between sessions. These deepen the session's impact. If you weren't able to do them, that itself is useful material to discuss.
      - baslik: You can end therapy whenever you choose
        metin: Therapy is not an endless obligation. When you have achieved your goals or simply no longer wish to continue, you can close the process healthily by informing your psychologist. A good psychologist will not try to keep you — they will respect your decision.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Therapy is not something only "weak" people do
    metin: On the contrary, going to therapy is a decision to invest in your own mental health. Millions of athletes, executives, artists and ordinary people around the world use therapy as a tool for growth. Going when there is no acute problem is also extremely valuable.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: How frequent should sessions be?
    metin: One session per week is the most common starting point. Fortnightly sessions can also be effective given time or budget constraints. You and your psychologist can decide together on the frequency that best suits your needs.
